            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Tamil for a long time, then the Tamil for ancestral roots."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: மன்னர்",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "மன்னர் — maṉṉar — \"a king\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "மன்னர் தன் மக்களை நன்றாகக் கவனித்துக்கொண்டார். maṉṉar taṉ makkaḷai naṉṟākak kavaṉittukkoṇṭār. The king took good care of his people."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "A respectful form; மன்னன் is the plain one."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "மன்னர் (maṉṉar) is native Dravidian: DEDR 4774 gives மன் and மன்னன், \"king, lord, chief\", and மன்னர் is its respectful form."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for telling what happened."
            }
          ]
